---
import SabGuide from '../../components/sab/SabGuide.astro';
import { getGuide } from '../../lib/guides';

const guide = getGuide('local-scholarships-better-odds');
---

<SabGuide guide={guide}>
  <p>
    A $70,000 national scholarship gets tens of thousands of applicants. A $1,000 award from a Medicine Hat service club might get twenty. Students chase the first kind and ignore the second, which is exactly backwards if the goal is money for school rather than a line for a resume.
  </p>

  <h2>The arithmetic</h2>
  <p>
    Suppose a national award pays $10,000 and draws 10,000 applicants, while a local award pays $1,000 and draws 25. The expected value of the national application is a dollar; the local one is forty. The local award also takes less time to apply for, and the same essay and reference letter usually work for several of them. Applying to five local awards is a few evenings of work with genuinely good odds; a national award is a long shot that costs a month.
  </p>
  <p>
    None of that is an argument for skipping the big ones if you are a real candidate for them. Apply to those too, and stop treating the small ones as the consolation round.
  </p>

  <h2>Why local committees are easier to win over</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>They know your context.</strong> A national reader does not know your school or what it means to hold a job in a small town. A local committee does, and often knows your referee personally.</li>
    <li><strong>They reward showing up.</strong> Many local awards go under-applied. Some years the applicant pool is in single digits. A complete, sincere application can win almost by arriving.</li>
    <li><strong>They value ordinary evidence.</strong> Local committees read part-time jobs, farm work, coaching, and volunteering as the point, not as filler.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where local awards hide</h2>
  <p>
    The catch: local awards are badly advertised. They live on photocopied sheets in guidance offices and outdated municipal webpages, which is the problem <a href="/about/">ScholarAB exists to fix</a>. The usual sources:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Your guidance office.</strong> Ask for the local awards list directly; some awards are only distributed through schools.</li>
    <li><strong>City and county programs</strong>, like <a href="/scholarships/cypress-county-agricultural-service-board-bursary/">Cypress County's two Agricultural Service Board bursaries</a> or the <a href="/scholarships/lethbridge-county-community-benefit-scholarship/">Lethbridge County Community Benefit Scholarship</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Service clubs</strong>: Rotary, Lions, Elks, Legion branches and their auxiliaries. The <a href="/scholarships/kin-canada-bursary/">Kin Canada Bursary</a>, endorsed by your nearest Kin club, is typical of the shape.</li>
    <li><strong>Community foundations</strong> that administer dozens of small named funds through a single application. Check whether the one near you actually funds students, though: plenty of them, including the Community Foundation of Southeastern Alberta, give only to charities.</li>
    <li><strong>Employers and unions</strong>, both yours and your parents'. Co-op and credit-union memberships often carry student awards; <a href="/scholarships/south-country-co-op-scholarship/">South Country Co-op</a> and the <a href="/scholarships/ata-local-21-scholarship/">ATA Local 21 Scholarship</a> are two you can look at right now.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    In <a href="/scholarships/">the directory</a>, listings are tagged by region, and the SCOPE row has a chip for each one: <a href="/scholarships/medicine-hat/">Medicine Hat</a>, <a href="/scholarships/calgary/">Calgary</a>, <a href="/scholarships/edmonton/">Edmonton</a>, <a href="/scholarships/lethbridge/">Lethbridge</a>, <a href="/scholarships/red-deer/">Red Deer</a> and Alberta-wide. Each city also has its own page, linked here, so you can see your whole local pool without touching a filter, and <a href="/match/">the match quiz</a> sorts it by what you qualify for.
  </p>

  <h2>The strategy in one paragraph</h2>
  <p>
    Apply to the <a href="/guides/alexander-rutherford-scholarship-guide/">Rutherford</a> because it is guaranteed money for marks you already have. Apply to every local award you plausibly qualify for, reusing one strong <a href="/guides/how-to-write-a-scholarship-essay/">essay</a> and one <a href="/guides/reference-letters-for-scholarships/">reference package</a>. Add national awards on top only where you are genuinely competitive. Small awards stack, and $1,000 three times is a semester's tuition.
  </p>
</SabGuide>
